{
  "category": "comparison",
  "goals": [
    {
      "id": "comparison-01",
      "goal": "tokio vs async-std: which one is still maintained and what differs?",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "The async-std README states that async-std has been discontinued.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/async-rs/async-std/main/README.md",
          "volatility": "slow",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "The async-std README recommends that users switch to smol instead.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/async-rs/async-std/main/README.md",
          "volatility": "slow",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "async-std was created to demonstrate an async library that stays as close to `std` as possible.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/async-rs/async-std/main/README.md",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Tokio provides a multithreaded, work-stealing based task scheduler.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tokio-rs/tokio/master/README.md",
          "volatility": "slow",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "smol is described as a small and fast async runtime.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/smol-rs/smol/master/README.md",
          "volatility": "slow",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Tokio-based libraries can be used with smol by applying the `async-compat` adapter.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/smol-rs/smol/master/README.md",
          "volatility": "slow",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-02",
      "goal": "Rust Rc vs Arc: when should I use each?",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "`Rc` is a single-threaded reference-counting pointer.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/rc/struct.Rc.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`Arc` is a thread-safe reference-counting pointer.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`Arc` uses atomic operations for its reference counting, which are more expensive than ordinary memory accesses.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "The docs recommend `Rc<T>` for lower overhead when reference-counted allocations are not shared between threads.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "The compiler rejects attempts to send an `Rc<T>` between threads.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`Arc<T>` implements `Send` and `Sync` only if `T` implements `Send` and `Sync`.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`Arc` stands for Atomically Reference Counted.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-03",
      "goal": "PostgreSQL json vs jsonb: what are the differences?",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "The `json` type stores an exact copy of the input text.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Processing functions must reparse `json` data on each execution.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`jsonb` data is stored in a decomposed binary format.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`jsonb` is slightly slower to input than `json` due to conversion overhead.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`jsonb` is significantly faster to process than `json` because no reparsing is needed.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`jsonb` supports indexing, which the docs call a significant advantage.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`jsonb` does not preserve the order of object keys.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`jsonb` keeps only the last value when the input has duplicate object keys.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "The docs say most applications should prefer storing JSON data as `jsonb` unless they have specialized needs.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html",
          "volatility": "slow",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-04",
      "goal": "HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3: what differs in the transport and header compression?",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "HTTP/3 maps HTTP semantics over the QUIC transport protocol.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "HTTP/2 has been used primarily with TLS over TCP.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "HTTP/3 replaces HTTP/2's HPACK header compression with QPACK.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "QPACK uses separate unidirectional streams to modify and track field table state.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "HPACK relies on in-order transmission of compressed field sections, a guarantee QUIC does not provide.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "HTTP/3 is selected with the \"h3\" ALPN token during the TLS handshake.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "An origin can advertise an HTTP/3 endpoint through the `Alt-Svc` response header field.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "If a QUIC connection fails because UDP is blocked, HTTP/3 clients SHOULD attempt a TCP-based version of HTTP.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "HTTP/2 uses binary message framing.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "HTTP/2 allows interleaving of messages from multiple exchanges on the same connection.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-05",
      "goal": "git merge vs git rebase: how do they integrate branches and when should I avoid rebase?",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "When histories have diverged, git merge performs a three-way merge using the two branch-tip snapshots and their common ancestor.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Basic-Branching-and-Merging",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "A merge commit is special in that it has more than one parent.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Basic-Branching-and-Merging",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "If the branch being merged is directly ahead of the current commit, git merge just moves the branch pointer forward (a fast-forward).",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Basic-Branching-and-Merging",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "git rebase takes the changes committed on one branch and replays them on top of another branch.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Rebasing",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "A rebased branch has a linear-looking history, as if all the work happened in series.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Rebasing",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Rebasing abandons the existing commits and creates new commits that are similar but different.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Rebasing",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Do not rebase commits that exist outside your repository and that people may have based work on.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Rebasing",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-06",
      "goal": "Python list vs tuple: mutability and typical use",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "Python tuples are immutable.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Python lists are mutable.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Assigning to an item of a tuple raises `TypeError`.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Tuples usually contain a heterogeneous sequence of elements.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "List elements are usually homogeneous.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "Tuple elements are usually accessed via unpacking or indexing.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "List elements are usually accessed by iterating over the list.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "A tuple can contain mutable objects, such as lists.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-07",
      "goal": "Rust HashMap vs BTreeMap: ordering, key requirements and when to pick which",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "`HashMap` iterates its keys and entries in arbitrary order.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.HashMap.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`HashMap` keys must implement the `Eq` and `Hash` traits.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.HashMap.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`BTreeMap` stores its entries in key order.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.BTreeMap.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`BTreeMap` keys must implement the `Ord` trait.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.BTreeMap.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "The std::collections guide says to use a `BTreeMap` when you want a map sorted by its keys.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/index.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "The std::collections guide says to use a `BTreeMap` when you want to get a range of entries on demand.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/index.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`BTreeMap` is an ordered map based on a B-Tree.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.BTreeMap.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison-08",
      "goal": "Rust Mutex vs RwLock: differences in locking and poisoning",
      "nuggets": [
        {
          "text": "An `RwLock` allows a number of readers or at most one writer at any point in time.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.RwLock.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "A `Mutex` does not distinguish between readers and writers that acquire the lock.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.RwLock.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`RwLock` does not guarantee any particular priority policy, which depends on the underlying operating system.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.RwLock.html",
          "volatility": "slow",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "A `Mutex` becomes poisoned if the thread holding it panics.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Mutex.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "The `lock` and `try_lock` methods of a `Mutex` return a `Result` indicating whether it is poisoned.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Mutex.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "An `RwLock` can only be poisoned if a panic occurs while it is locked exclusively in write mode.",
          "scope": "core",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.RwLock.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`Mutex<T>` is `Sync` when `T: Send`.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Mutex.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        },
        {
          "text": "`RwLock<T>` is `Sync` only when `T: Send + Sync`.",
          "scope": "supporting",
          "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.RwLock.html",
          "volatility": "never",
          "verified_on": "2026-10-08"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
